WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.840
Detection depends on knowing what normal looks like.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.220
A sudden spike does not automatically mean a hostile event.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.240
It could be a marketing campaign, a product launch, a news story, a crawler,

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.660
an integration mistake, a retry storm, a failed cache, or a DDoS event.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.280
Baselines help responders compare current behavior against expected patterns for the

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.380
service,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.440
day of week, season, region, and business event.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.060
Without baselines, teams argue from memory while the system is already struggling.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.840
Useful signals include requests per second, bandwidth, packets per second,

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:43.460
connection rates, error rates, latency, queue depth, CPU, memory, cache hit rate,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:50.000
database pressure, load balancer health, WAF events, DNS query rates, and provider alerts.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.900
No single metric is enough.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.040
A volumetric event may be obvious in bandwidth and provider telemetry.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.420
A state exhaustion event may show connection pressure and device limits.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.440
An application-layer event may show normal-looking request rates but abnormal errors,

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.780
expensive routes, or saturation behind the web tier.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.240
Edge and provider telemetry are especially important because local infrastructure may only

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.060
see the traffic that survives upstream filtering or edge routing.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.800
During a large event,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.620
the origin can look quiet because the provider is absorbing pressure,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.160
or it can look overloaded because the edge is passing traffic that should be controlled

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:28.180
earlier.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.280
Responders should know where to find CDN logs, WAF events, DNS analytics,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.900
scrubbing dashboards, ISP tickets, and cloud provider notifications.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.800
Dashboards should support classification, not just alert volume.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.980
A good DDoS dashboard helps the team answer practical questions: what changed,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.440
where is pressure appearing, which services are affected, which users are harmed,

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.900
which controls are active, and whether the response is improving availability.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.480
Alerts should be tuned to business patterns and service criticality.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:05.140
During response, the best updates are factual: observed signal, likely impact,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.880
current action, next checkpoint, and confidence level.
